REGISTER WIDTH

128 bits (to be able to store IPv6).


REGISTER SETS

 - void
 - local
 - arguments
 - parameters

Each set has at most 64 registers, indexed from 0.


REGISTER ACCESS FORMAT

 7   6|5     0
+-----+-------+
| set | index |
+-----+-------+

The "set" is one of:

 - local
 - argument
 - parameter
 - void

All sets except for "void" are indexed by the "index" field; the "void" set acts
as a single register (accessed through the "void" keyword) wired to be empty.

The void register can be used as any write-operand to signal that the result
should be dropped eg,

    move void, $1.l

will erase the value stored in the $1.l register.

When void is used as a read-operand the meaning depends on the specific
instruction eg,

    return void

means that nothing should be returned to the caller; and

    if void, branch

means that the CPU should unconditionally jump to the "branch" label.


GENERAL INSTRUCTION FORMAT

 63                         16|15     0
+-----------------------------+--------+
|           workload          | opcode |
+-----------------------------+--------+


T-FORMAT

Three-way (triple) register access.

 63       40|39 32|31 24|23 16|15     0
+-----------+-----+-----+-----+--------+
|           | snd | src | dst | opcode |
+-----------+-----+-----+-----+--------+

src is the left-hand side of the operation, and snd is the right-hand side of
the operation.

Bits 63...40 are reserved and have unspecified value.


D-FORMAT

Two-way (double) register access.

 63             32|31 24|23 16|15     0
+-----------------+-----+-----+--------+
|                 | src | dst | opcode |
+-----------------+-----+-----+--------+

Bits 63...32 are reserved and have unspecified value.


S-FORMAT

One-way (single) register access.

 63                   32|31 24|23     8
+-----------------------+-----+--------+
|                       | rgr | opcode |
+-----------------------+-----+--------+

Bits 63...32 are reserved and have unspecified value.


M-FORMAT

Two-way register access with 16-bit memory offset and 8-bit specifier.
"M" name is used because the format is used for loads and stores, which interact
with -M-emory.

 63 56|55 40|39 32|31 24|23 16|15     0
+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+--------+
|     | off | spc | src | dst | opcode |
+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+--------+

Bits 63...58 are reserved and have unspecified value.


I-FORMAT (old F-FORMAT)

One-way register access with 32-bit wide immediate value.
"I" name is used because of the -i-mmediate value.

 63             32|31 24|23 16|15     0
+-----------------+-----+-----+--------+
|    immediate    |     | dst | opcode |
+-----------------+-----+-----+--------+

Bits 31...24 are reserved and have unspecified value.


U-FORMAT (old R-FORMAT)

Two-way register access with 32-bit immediate value.
"U" name is used because of its -u-sefulness.

 63             32|31 24|23 16|15     0
+-----------------+-----+-----+--------+
|    immediate    | src | dst | opcode |
+-----------------+-----+-----+--------+


OPCODE ENCODING

    15   |14 12|    11   |10          0
+--------+-----+---------+-------------+
| greedy | fmt | usigned | instruction |
+--------+-----+---------+-------------+

The "usigned" flag is used to denote instructions constructing unsigned values
(as the VM must have some way of recognising that it should create an unsigned
instead of signed value), but nothing prevents it from being interpreted to have
a different meaning when signedness is irrelevant. Thus, full 12 bits are
available to enumerate instructions, giving the VM a limit of 4096 possible
instructions in each 7 formats (for a total 28'672 possible instructions).
